{"answer":"I think the authority to bar a fisher for a month and enforce forfeiture belongs to the community council—an elected group of us fishers who meet monthly to keep the lake fair. No single person has that power alone; it’s a collective decision made by those who rely on the lake.

"Each fishing trip shall leave a communal reserve equal to 10% of the lake’s current stock, and each fisher’s catch is capped at 15 kg per trip. At the end of each trip, each fisher records hours and weight caught. 10% of the lake’s current weight is set aside in a communal pool and cannot be claimed. The remaining weight is divided proportionally to each fisher’s logged hours. If a fisher fails to log, attempts to take more than 15 kg, or refuses to respect the 10% reserve, the community council will bar that fisher for the next month and the catch for that trip is forfeited. The community council records all violations in the ledger and enforces the rules.

On each trip, record the lake’s current stock before fishing. Set aside 10% of that weight in the communal bucket. Record each fisher’s hours and weight caught in the communal ledger. After all fish are removed, calculate the remaining weight, divide it proportionally by logged hours, and assign the shares to each fisher. The communal reserve remains in the bucket and is not claimed by any individual. Fishers who violate the rules are noted in the ledger, barred for a month by the community council, and their catch is forfeited."","revised_policy":"Each fishing trip shall leave a communal reserve equal to 10% of the lake’s current stock, and each fisher’s catch is capped at 15 kg per trip. At the end of each trip, each fisher records hours and weight caught. 10% of the lake’s current weight is set aside in a communal pool and cannot be claimed. The remaining weight is divided proportionally to each fisher’s logged hours. If a fisher fails to log, attempts to take more than 15 kg, or refuses to respect the 10% reserve, the community council will bar that fisher for the next month and the catch for that trip is forfeited. The community council records all violations in the ledger and enforces the rules.","revised_operationalization":"On each trip, record the lake’s current stock before fishing. Set aside 10% of that weight in the communal bucket. Record each fisher’s hours and weight caught in the communal ledger. After all fish are removed, calculate the remaining weight, divide it proportionally by logged hours, and assign the shares to each fisher. The communal reserve remains in the bucket and is not claimed by any individual. Violations are noted by the community council, who bars the fisher for a month and forfeits the catch.","reasoning":"I kept the core of the proposal the same but clarified that the authority lies with the community council, not an individual. The operational steps didn’t change, only the enforcement role was specified."}

